{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984 + spec/interaction-events.md:79: while the run is held open EXPLICITLY, a handler that takes longer than its own interval `degrades to running back to back` (spec/interaction-events.md:189-196) and is bounded by the ordinary instruction budget, which raises `ol-limit` exactly as it does for any other non-terminating program. This is the counterpart of `every-queued-occurrence-discarded-when-run-closes`: there the main line ends and the queue is discarded, here the main line never ends because the learner wrote `forever`, so every queued occurrence is drained at the next checkpoint and the handler never catches up. Both fixtures are needed, and neither alone is sufficient: the discard rule on its own could be satisfied by never draining at all -- the exact defect this issue's first implementation shipped -- and this fixture is what forbids that. Note the discriminator is the EVENT SEQUENCE, not the diagnostic: `forever` exhausts the budget under every reading, so `ol-limit` appears either way. What differs is how many handler firings precede it -- 15 here, and a different count under each of the three rejected readings measured below. That discrimination is re-derived at THIS fixture's current budget of 111, by mutation rather than by assertion: with the missed occurrence never queued the fixture FAILS; with the tick-dispatch drain of already-queued occurrences disabled it FAILS; and with the main-line boundary draining in a loop until its queue empties it FAILS. Each of those three mutations was verified to have applied in `packages/runtime/src` AND to have reached `packages/runtime/dist` (content hash plus an explicit post-emit pattern) before its result was believed, and the unmutated fixture passes. Two readings named before issue #953 -- a strict fixed-delay clock, and a boundary that also drains after the last statement -- are NOT among those three and are not claimed here; they were measured only under the pre-#953 accounting at the budget this fixture then used, so no current number is asserted for them. The direction of the difference is not the point and it is not uniform; the fold differing is -- and a conformance fixture folds the whole stream, so those counts are what fail. A small `instructionBudget` keeps the trace readable while pinning the same property a default budget would. Issue #953 made each `wait` tick a charged instruction, which this program pays twice over (the handler's `wait 3` and the `forever` body's `wait 1`), so the budget was re-derived from 60 to 111 -- the value at which the run folds to the BYTE-IDENTICAL event stream recorded below, with the same 15 firings and the same ol-limit source span; only `params.value`, which reports the budget itself, moved.",
